//? Title: RealNVP: Per-Point Volume Change and Exact-Form Inversion
//? Subtitle: Bounded affine couplings, inverse-path determinants and a preserved banana fit
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An invertible density model must assign normalized probabilities to observations
and generate new observations from a simple base distribution. Affine couplings
make both operations tractable while allowing local volume change. This native
C++ study checks one hand-set coupling before fitting the original four-stage
bounded RealNVP to procedural banana pools. The complete program retains all
48 Adam writes, both actual Jacobians, per-point forward and inverse-path log
determinants, and all 1,024 latent-only samples. The fixed-final test NLL is
2.879116774 nats per two-coordinate row, better than the fitting-only full
Gaussian's 2.994631823, while analytic truth scores 2.796480843. Exact source
reproduction checks this compact experiment; it is not the full image model
or a state-of-the-art density benchmark.
)
\toc

> Allow local volume change with triangular affine couplings

[Dinh, Sohl-Dickstein and Bengio, Density estimation using Real NVP
(2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.08803) uses invertible affine couplings with
tractable likelihoods. Split coordinates into a,b and preserve a:

$$ y_A=a,\quad y_B=b\exp(s(a))+t(a),\qquad
J=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\b\exp(s)s'(a)+t'(a)&\exp(s)\end{pmatrix}. $$
\alttext(The affine coupling keeps a and scales and shifts b using functions only of a. Its triangular Jacobian has determinant exp(s(a)).)

Thus log|det J|=s(a). Invert with a=y_A and b=(y_B−t(a))exp(−s(a));
the inverse logdet is −s(a), evaluated on that inverse path's conditioning
coordinate. Reversing several stages requires recomputing each conditioner
on its current inverse input, not reusing values from an unrelated forward pass.
The compact source uses four alternating 1->16 tanh->2 conditioners, with
**s=.8 tanh(raw_scale)** and t the second head. This bounded CPU adaptation
omits the paper's image multiscale architecture and batch normalization.

A final global positive diagonal scale gives z=exp(log_scale)×h. Accumulate
four per-row coupling logdets in order, then add sum(log_scale):

$$ \log p_X(x)=-\log(2\pi)-\tfrac12\|z\|^2+
\sum_{i=0}^3s_i(h_i)+\sum_{c=0}^1\mathrm{log\_scale}_c. $$
\alttext(The observation log density is Gaussian latent log density plus the four input-dependent coupling log determinants and the global scale log determinant.)

The loss is negative mean log density over rows, in nats per two-dimensional
observation. Sampling supplies Gaussian z, unscales it, then reverses blocks
3,2,1,0. No rejection, ODE solve, approximate determinant or fitting loop is
hidden inside the model API. Actual input Jacobians of both maps check the
analytic determinants independently of the Gaussian density calculation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rnvp_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=32;
 d.node("split",{-24,0},"Coordinates a,b\nCondition on a",s)
 .node("shift",{-11,0},"Heads s(a),t(a)\n1 -> 16 tanh -> 2",s)
 .node("add",{2,0},"Keep a\nb exp(s) + t",s)
 .node("stack",{15,0},"4 affine blocks\nPositive global scale",s)
 .node("density",{28,0},"Gaussian base\nAdd per-row logdet",s)
 .edge("split","shift").edge("shift","add").edge("add","stack").edge("stack","density")
 .save_svg("build/rnvp_method.svg");
}
